# Third-party sources

A page being public does not make it an allowed source. A third-party page, book, talk, or repository counts under [clean-room.mdc](clean-room.mdc) only when its own information did not come from Windows source, leaked NT source, the Windows Research Kernel, disassembly, decompiler output, Microsoft debug symbols, or WDK or SDK headers.

These are not sources. Do not open them for this tree:

- Geoff Chappell's Windows pages (geoffchappell.com)
- Vergilius Project and other struct dumps generated from Microsoft symbols
- Syscall number tables built from Microsoft binaries, such as j00ru's tables
- ReactOS-adjacent wikis or posts that quote leaked source or the Windows Research Kernel
- Blog posts, write-ups, or videos that walk through Microsoft code in a disassembler or debugger
- Undocumented-API sites whose prototypes or layouts come from symbols or disassembly

When a page does not say where its information came from, treat it as not a source. Use a Learn URL, a public specification, code already in this tree, or a guest log instead.

Wine and ReactOS code already in this tree, or pulled under `REACTOS_LINEAGE_IMPORTS`, stays allowed as in-tree code. Do not pull new code from any other Windows reimplementation project without Ryan's approval.

```text
Right: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/... for a documented DDI
Right: dll/win32/kernel32/client/file/create.c already in this tree
Wrong: a struct offset from Vergilius Project because it is public
Wrong: a syscall number from a public table built from ntdll.dll
```
